2026-11-14, the same pattern in an evaluation kit (the routed read on Orchard): six pilots in about ten hours, each stopped by one defect (a pre-existing run folder; a gate probe the model was never trained on; a logit tolerance below bfloat16 resolution; an uploaded archive with stale files; a second stale hash in another asset; then, on the first real items, a candidate list that refused 136 of 150 distance items for both arms and a decode with no repetition stop). Each pilot cost 1 to 1.5 hours of fix, reseal and queue. Two independent Claude passes each took about 15 minutes and found what the next pilots would have found: an audit of every hash and identity check in the job's path against the uploaded bytes, and a CPU replay of the scripted arm over all items. I ordered both only after the fifth failure, and my own narrowed scope of the lane's local check caused the fifth. For any new job family, before the first submission: (1) an independent audit of every pin and check along the job's path against the exact uploaded objects; (2) a CPU replay of whatever part of the read needs no model (reference arm, admission lists, limits) over the full item set; (3) make the first GPU job reach real items of every type, not only a gate; (4) when I compare two seals, question any changed file whose copies or pins did not all move.
EOF
